From e59173afcf1c78e28247889e96a08c20f54f3d98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 21:51:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] export the size of the thread descriptor
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: A leak checker has to scan the thread descriptor as a root: the libc
state a thread carries lives there, and an allocation whose only reference
is in it otherwise looks unreachable.  The descriptor sits at (variant II)
or just below (variant I) the thread pointer, but its size is an
implementation detail that differs per architecture and changes between
versions, so an out-of-tree tool cannot compute it and must not hardcode
it.

Export it as __pthread_struct_size.  It goes in __init_tls.c because that
translation unit is pulled into every static link (__libc_start_main calls
__init_tls), so a weak reference from outside always finds it.
---
 src/env/__init_tls.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/env/__init_tls.c b/src/env/__init_tls.c
index a93141ed..acf923fa 100644
--- a/src/env/__init_tls.c
+++ b/src/env/__init_tls.c
@@ -11,6 +11,15 @@
 
 volatile int __thread_list_lock;
 
+/* Size of the thread descriptor, which lives at (variant II) or just below
+ * (variant I) the thread pointer.  It is not part of any public interface and
+ * differs per architecture and per version, so a tool that has to know where
+ * the descriptor is cannot compute or hardcode it.  A leak checker needs it:
+ * everything a thread holds in libc state lives in the descriptor, and an
+ * allocation referenced only from there looks unreachable unless that memory
+ * is scanned as a root. */
+const size_t __pthread_struct_size = sizeof(struct pthread);
+
 int __init_tp(void *p)
 {
 	pthread_t td = p;
